      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1953 bulldozers graded 1,270 acres of farmland for Lincoln Village, adding churches, a school, a library, and a wooded park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lincoln_Village%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The community began in 1955 as a planned suburb by Nationwide Insurance’s real‑estate arm, aiming to house 10,000 residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lincoln_Village%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Pennsylvania Railroad ran along Lincoln Village’s northern edge, while Interstate 270 borders it to the east.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
